 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method enables training on a single GPU by splitting the scene into blocks and sharing the decoder. This reduces memory requirements compared to training a single large model with a larger hash table (e.g., $2^{21}$) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the Tanks and Temples dataset, SCALAR-NeRF with a hash table size of $2^{19}$ surpasses Block-NeRF and Instant-NGP with a hash table size of $2^{21}$ in terms of PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Scalability for large-scale scenes, ability to train on a single GPU, avoidance of foggy artifacts in overlapping regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires KMeans clustering for block partitioning. The quality of the coarse global model impacts the final fusion [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Large-scale scene reconstruction where memory is limited and foggy artifacts from block blending are a concern [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Small-scale scenes where a single global model fits in memory, or when high-speed training is prioritized over reconstruction quality (though SCALAR-NeRF is also fast due to fixed decoder) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method allows training on a single GPU by splitting the scene into blocks, avoiding the need for a large hash table size (e.g., $2^{21}$) which would be infeasible on non-commercial GPUs [S1, unofficial]. The hash table size is $2^{19}$, which is smaller than Instant-NGP's $2^{21}$ but still surpasses its performance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the Tanks and Temples dataset, SCALAR-NeRF outperforms Block-NeRF and Instant-NGP (with hash table size $2^{21}$) in PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS [S1, unofficial]. It achieves higher quality in detailed areas even with a smaller hash table size [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** The method scales to large-scale scenes by decomposing them into manageable blocks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Avoids the 'foggy' issue of Block-NeRF; enables single-GPU training for large scenes; superior scalability in reconstruction quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the AABB scale factor ($s_{\text{AABB}}$); a too-large factor increases hash collisions and degrades quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Large-scale scene reconstruction where single-GPU resources are limited and Block-NeRF's foggy artifacts are problematic [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes where standard NeRF or Instant-NGP suffice; scenes where blocks are very close together and IDW fusion is mistakenly used (SCALAR-NeRF's global model fusion is required) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-SCALAR-NeRF was introduced by Yu Chen and Gim Hee Lee from the Department of Computer Science, National University of Singapore, in November 2023 [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the challenges of large-scale neural scene reconstruction, specifically the limitations of Block-NeRF (foggy appearance) and the memory constraints of increasing hash table sizes in methods like Instant-NGP [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the encoder-decoder architecture of NeRF and the multi-resolution hash encoding of Instant-NGP [S1, unofficial].
